===== final2.patch (sha256 dc0dbd0bf78af1c9fd2e9803c44bd7bb0de72c4915daa0b8f452cc24c0820951): the diff of the four files the change touches, as applied on the VM


# Qwen3.6 on a Colab L4, pure Vulkan: origin/dev against the change (2026-10-27)
# Question (written before the runs): on a 24 GB card, what does the start-up probe of this change say and cost, and is the change
#   harmless where the window is enough? What came out was not a comparison of speeds: origin/dev did not finish its start-up in
#   900 s, and diag.sh was written afterwards to find out why. Its questions, written before its runs:
#   d0 how long does one refused block cost once the host-visible device-local type is full? d1 does dev start with
#   COLI_VK_STAGED=1 (the window is the cause, not something else of the tree)? d2 does the change with COLI_VK_STAGED=0
#   (the mapped path, as dev) stay in its start-up as dev does?
# Hardware: a Colab L4 high-mem VM: NVIDIA L4 (23034 MiB, driver 580.82.07, BAR1 32768 MiB, in bar1.txt below), Vulkan through the
#   driver's EGL library (an ICD json on libEGL_nvidia.so.0), 12 threads, 52 GiB of RAM, the model on /content.
# Model: Kreuzzelg/qwen36-35b-a3b-colibri-i4-gs64 (Hugging Face), Qwen3.6 35B-A3B, experts int4-g64.
# Engines, cloned and built on the VM with make -C c qwen36 VK=1 (no CUDA). "dev2" in the names below is the origin/dev tree
#   (not COLI_VK_DEV2); "win" is the tree with the change:
#   A (dev2)  origin/dev c1c06ac6fcb32157e4da130fd96775a32a757052, qwen36 sha256 eb657effa4d8e3f84acfdca5f4582d125f1af708170c216178bd9f279eeb7344
#   B (win)   the same tree with this change, qwen36 sha256 c9940dba960711423220ef4c34cbc7d51286eb1a380a8074ec64f32ac725c871
#   The patch applied to the tree: sha256 116a9767d8666aaa4e5ac3eb5129d0540cfb2226e8e7050c654a4d6668fecff5 (the A100 record's).
# Each run, a new process from the tree's root:
#   env COLI_VULKAN=1 COLI_NO_OMP_TUNE=1 OMP_NUM_THREADS=6 SNAP=/content/q36 N_NEW=32 /usr/bin/time -v ./c/qwen36 256 4 <prompt>
#   under timeout 900 (240 for d2), with nvidia-smi every 2 s beside it. Prompts: p64 and p512, plain-text prompts of 64 and
#   512 tokens ("[enc] prompt tokens" in the logs); their hashes were not recorded on the L4. 32 new tokens, free generation: the
#   tok/s is of 32 tokens, only to see that the run works, not a speed measure.
# History: it was NOT fixed on the L4. The script copies a saved history before each run when one exists, but the warm-up (w) did
#   not finish, so none was saved, and every run started from the history the earlier runs left in the model folder. The [USAGE]
#   lines say how big it was (none for w and p1, 30400 selections for p3, 60800 for p4/q1/q2, 234560 for q3, 408320 for q4/d1,
#   438720 for d2) and the warm-start line how many of the 10240 experts it ordered (0 for p2, 3882 for p3 and q2, 6071 for q3 and
#   d1). It sets which experts the tier takes first, so read the first-token times (p2 0.84 s with no history, p3 0.30 s with some)
#   with that in mind.
# Runs, in order: w, p1-dev2, p2-win, p3-win, p4-dev2 (60 s of rest between them), q1-dev2, q2-win, q3-win, q4-dev2, then diag.sh:
#   d0-sonda5, d1-dev2staged, d2-win-mapped. w, p1, p4 and q1 (dev, mapped path) were stopped by the 900 s timeout while still in
#   the expert tier's start-up (the log ends at "int4 packed weights detected"; the GPU holds 10489 MiB and shows 0 to 20 % use
#   for the whole 900 s). q4-dev2 was stopped by hand (pkill, rc 143) 91 s after it started, after a backtrace had been taken.
#   Backtraces (gdb -p, thread apply all bt): of q4, seen on screen, not kept in a file: four tier threads in pool_suballoc
#   waiting for the pool's mutex, one thread inside the driver (an ioctl in libnvidia-eglcore), the main thread in the uploader.
#   Of d2-win-mapped, a minute into its start-up, kept below (d2-gdb.txt): four threads in pool_suballoc and the main thread in
#   pool_free_range, all waiting for the pool's mutex, and one thread inside the driver. In backend_vulkan.c, pool_suballoc holds the
#   pool's mutex while pool_new_block calls vkAllocateMemory, and a refused block is counted (refusals++) but asked again for the
#   next tensor (read in the code; no log of a stuck start-up counts its refusals). d0-sonda5 is a separate program: a refused
#   block took 0.59 to 0.65 s in it.
#   No run of dev finished, so no speed of dev on this card exists; the p2/p3 and q2/q3 rows are the change's.
