# PART 1 (sonda is Italian for probe): a first A100 VM of the same kind as runs.txt's (NVIDIA A100-SXM4-80GB, driver 580.82.07, the same ICD), 2026-10-26, before the runs of the earlier version of this change. sonda2.c, sonda3.c
#   and sonda4.c are small C programs written for this question: sonda2.c allocates blocks the way the weight pool does
#   (backend_vulkan.c pool_new_block: 256 MiB or 1 GiB blocks, the device-address flag, not mapped) in the host-visible
#   device-local type until the driver refuses, then one big block, then the device-only type. sonda3.c and sonda4.c
#   are the same program grown in two steps: they make the device with or without bufferDeviceAddress and
#   VK_EXT_memory_budget (BDA=1, BUDGET=1), sonda4.c also with a CUDA context, the allocation flag and the mapping
#   (CUDA=1, FLAG=1, MAP=1), to see whether any of them changes where the driver stops; the variants are named in their
#   outputs. All three sources are below. Built and run on the VM with:
#   gcc -O1 -Wall -o sonda sonda.c -lvulkan && LD_LIBRARY_PATH=/usr/lib64-nvidia VK_DRIVER_FILES=<icd> ./sonda
#   (sonda4.c with -lcudart for the CUDA variants).
